      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Some nebulized bronchodilator solutions contain additives, such as EDTA, benzalkonium chloride (BAC), or both."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "Although BAC-induced bronchoconstriction has been well documented in patients with asthma, there is no information on the effects of EDTA on FEV(1) when inhaled in the amounts that would be administered during emergency department treatment of asthma."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Eighteen subjects with stable asthma and airway responsiveness to methacholine were randomly assigned to inhale up to four 600-microg nebulized doses of EDTA, BAC (positive control), and normal saline (placebo) in a double-blind crossover manner on separate days. FEV(1) was measured 15 minutes after each dose. Treatments were repeated every 20 minutes until FEV(1) decreased by 20% or greater or a maximum of 4 doses were administered."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Mean +/- SD maximum percent decrease in FEV(1) was 1.8% +/- 5.8% after EDTA, 16.6% +/- 13.9% after BAC, and 3.6% +/- 8.2% after placebo (P <.001); there was no significant difference between EDTA and placebo."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The amount of EDTA contained in maximum recommended doses of nebulized bronchodilators does not induce bronchospasm. In contrast, BAC induces clinically important bronchospasm, which could decrease the efficacy of a bronchodilator during an emergency."
        }
      ]},
